=== P18.5: a CORRECTION to the validation-noise magnitude measurement ===
date: 2026-10-01T04:57:41Z

WHAT WAS FIRST REPORTED, at logs/12_validation_noise.log:
    max relative |delta l2| = 4.632e-02   (L36 records <= 4.1e-07)

That figure is WRONG, and it is wrong in the alarming direction. It appeared to contradict
KNOWN_LIMITATIONS L36 by five orders of magnitude. L36 is not contradicted; the INSTRUMENT
was broken.

THE DEFECT. The measurement grepped the l2 lines out of 'git diff -U0' and paired the values
with 'paste - -'. That is correct ONLY when the diff alternates -/+. When a hunk GROUPS its
removed lines before its added ones -- which it does whenever several adjacent keys change
together -- the pairing lines up grid 1's value with grid 2's value and reports the
REFINEMENT RATIO BETWEEN DIFFERENT GRIDS as if it were drift.

PROVEN ON A SYNTHETIC DIFF whose true answer was 1.0e-09, same values both ways:
    alternating -/+    reported 1.000e-09    correct
    grouped   --/++    reported 3.000e+00    wrong by nine orders of magnitude

THE REPLACEMENT: tools/compare_validation_json.py parses both JSON documents and compares
them BY KEY PATH, so a value is only ever compared with the same value. It separates timing
keys from the rest and fails closed if it compares 0 keys.

--- the replacement, run live against reports regenerated from the DEBUG build ---
    (MomentumMMS.UConverges re-run from build/qual-debug, 33.8 s, regenerating 3 JSON
     reports that are tracked in their Release-generated form)
    files compared     : 3
    numeric keys compared : 810
    keys absent from the new file : 0  (a structural change, not drift)
    
    non-timing keys: NONE CHANGED (max relative change = 0)
    max relative change, TIMING     : 1.394e+00  (expected, meaningless)
      at results/validation/mms/momentum_mms_upwind.json :: runtime.levels[0].seconds
    
    KNOWN_LIMITATIONS L36 records raw norms moving <= 4.1e-07 relative and
    Richardson-extrapolated quantities <= 1.1e-05, between Release and Debug builds.

SO FOR THIS TEST, A DEBUG RE-RUN MOVED NOTHING BUT TIMINGS -- the non-timing keys are
bitwise unchanged across 810 compared keys, which is stronger than L36's bound rather than
weaker. It does not generalise to all 50 files; the full-set magnitude for the P18.5 run is
recorded as NOT MEASURED, because the only measurement taken of it came from the broken
instrument and the regenerated files have since been restored.

WHAT THE RESTORE DECISION ACTUALLY RESTED ON, and still rests on, is the CLASSIFICATION at
logs/12_validation_noise.log, which is independent of the magnitude:
    37 distinct changed keys, every one in the timing/iteratively-converged allow-list
    10 physics fields checked, every one with 0 changed lines
    1076 changed lines inspected (the positive control against a blind verdict)

